  "articleBody": "It's incredibly daunting when you're suddenly facing federal criminal charges. The federal system can feel complex and intimidating, but knowing your rights and taking the right first steps can make a big difference. Your immediate priority should be to remain silent. Anything you say can and will be used against you, so it's best to wait until you have legal counsel. Don't try to explain your side of the story to law enforcement without an attorney present, no matter how innocent you feel. Next, resist the urge to destroy or alter any evidence, even if you think it's insignificant. This can lead to additional charges like obstruction of justice. It’s also wise to avoid discussing your case with anyone other than your attorney; this includes friends, family, or even on social media. These conversations aren't privileged and could potentially harm your defense. Finally, and most crucially, contact an experienced federal criminal defense attorney right away. They can explain the specific charges you're facing, guide you through the process, and start building your defense. Don't wait; the sooner you have legal representation, the better positioned you'll be to protect your future.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-26"
}